Here it is, and it works for rectangles too:

function boustrophedonGrid(width, height = width) {
  let count = 1;
  const grid = [];
  for (let row = 0; row < height; row++) {
    const r = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < width; i++) r.push(count++);
    if (row % 2) r.reverse();   // every other row runs right to left
    grid.push(r);
  }
  return grid.map(r => r.join(' ')).join('\n');
}

boustrophedonGrid(3) gives 1 2 3 / 6 5 4 / 7 8 9, and boustrophedonGrid(4, 2) gives 1 2 3 4 / 8 7 6 5.

One thing I couldn't do: true boustrophedon also mirrors the letters on reversed lines, which plain console text can't show. On a web page, CSS transform: scaleX(-1) on every other row would do it.
